reduced\ndrift on preservation metrics.\n1. INTRODUCTION\nDense retrieval underpins modern music search and rec-\nommendation systems, where tracks are embedded into\na shared space and retrieved by similarity to text or au-\ndio queries. For example, a user might search for “am-\nbient electronic music” or retrieve tracks similar to a ref-\nerence clip. While effective at capturing overall seman-\ntic similarity, these systems offer limited control over spe-\ncific attributes—such as instrumentation, mood, or produc-\n© J. Guinot, A. Riou, E. Quinton and G. Fazekas. Licensed\nunder a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY\n4.0). Attribution: J. Guinot, A. Riou, E. Quinton and G. Fazekas, “Steer-\ning dense music retrieval with open-vocabulary concept discovery”, in\nProc. of the 27th Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., Abu\nDhabi, UAE, 2026.\ntion characteristics—without altering the broader content\nof the query. This motivates steerable retrieval: modify-\ning the query embedding at retrieval time by amplifying\nor suppressing directions associated with a concept before\nnearest-neighbour search.\nSparse autoencoders (SAEs) provide a natural interface\nfor this problem. By forcing e\n\n=== PAGE 2 ===\n❄️\n🔥\n🔥\n(a) Training\n\"electric\nguitar\"\n❄️\n❄️\n.24\n.71\n.08\n(b) Discover-Then-Name cosine probing\n\"electric\nguitar\"\n🔥\n❄️\n❄️\n(c) Sparse Modality Inversion (ours)\nFigure 1: Overview of the proposed approach. (a) Unimodal SAEs are trained on 10-second music snippet embeddings\nza to decompose the dense embedding space into sparse codes s. (b) Discover-Then-Name consists in decoding one-hot\nvectors e1, . . . , em and picking the one, here d2, which aligns best with the concept embedding zc. (c) We instead derive\nconcept support via sparse inversion by jointly optimizing the distance ∥z −zc∥and the manifold regularization Γ(z, A).\noccupy exactly the same regions of the shared space [26].\nA text embedding for electric guitar may point toward the\nright semantic neighbourhood without coinciding with the\naudio embeddings of guitar examples. As a result, neu-\nrons that are most aligned with the concept on the text side\nmay not be the neurons active for audio examples express-\ning that concept. For steerable retrieval, we therefore need\nsparse supports that reflect how the concept is represented\nin audio embeddings (audio-faithful), not only how well\neach neuron matches the text query.\nWe study this problem in music retrieval and treat con-\ncept attribution as sparse support recovery rather than neu-\nron ranking. Instead of selecting neurons independently\nby text alignment, we solve a lightweight, training-free\ninverse problem: given a concept embedding, recover a\nsparse code whose decoded representation reconstructs the\nconcept while remaining close to the empirical audio dis-\ntribution. Figure 1 summarizes the approach. We train\nSAEs on audio embeddings from joint music–text en-\ncoders, show that cosine-based probing recovers supports\npoorly aligned with concept-bearing audio examples, and\nreplace this heuristic with sparse inversion under geometric\nand distributional constraints. We evaluate both attribution\nstrategies for steerable retrieval, including concept ampli-\nfication, suppression, and concept-specific comparison.\nOur contributions are threefold:\n• We formulate controllable music retrieval as sparse fea-\nture intervention in SAE spaces and identify concept at-\ntribution as the key obstacle to open-vocabulary control.\n• We show that standard attribution strategies are unreli-\nable in multimodal settings: concept support is often dis-\ntributed across multiple features, and text-side alignment\ndoes not recover audio-faithful sparse support.\n• We introduce a lightweight, training-free inversion\nmethod for mapping free-form concepts to audio-side\nsparse supports, and show that it yields more precise,\nmore manifold-faithful retrieval edits.\nCode 1 and examples are released. Additional examples\nare available on the companion website 2 .\n1 https://github.com/Pliploop/sparse-steer-retrieval/\n2 https://pliploop.github.io/sparse-steer-retrieval/\n2. BACKGROUND\n2.1 Sparse Autoencoders\nLet f : X →A ⊂Rd be a pretrained representation\nlearning model. A Sparse Autoen\n\n=== PAGE 3 ===\n0.4\n0.2\n0.0\n0.2\ncos (di, zc)\n0\n5\nDensity\nW = 0.938\nRock\n0.2\n0.0\n0.2\n0.4\ncos (di, zc)\nW = 0.967\nViolin\n0.2\n0.0\n0.2\n0.4\ncos (di, zc)\nW = 0.929\nCuba\n0.2\n0.0\n0.2\n0.4\ncos (di, zc)\nW = 0.976\nAdventure\n4\n2\n0\n2\n4\nTheoretical Quantiles\n0.4\n0.2\n0.0\n0.2\n0.4\nSample Quantiles\nOutliers\n( ,\n)\n4\n2\n0\n2\n4\nTheoretical Quantiles\n4\n2\n0\n2\n4\nTheoretical Quantiles\n4\n2\n0\n2\n4\nTheoretical Quantiles\n(a) Cosine similarity distributions for Rock, Violin, Cuba and Adventure.\n0\n10\n20\n30\n40\n50\n|\n(C)|\n0\n10\n20\n30\n40\n50\n60\nNumber of concepts\n(b) Concept cardinalities under 4σ filtering.\nFigure 2: (a) Distribution of cosine similarity between zc and decoder atoms di for selected concepts using a BatchTopK\nSAE trained on MuQ embeddings with L0 = 10. Red line shows Fitted Gaussian and W ∈[0, 1] is the Shapiro-Wilks test\nstatistic for normality [31]. (b) Histogram of concept cardinalities |K(c)| obtained by filtering cosine similarities outside\nthe 4σ confidence interval, showing variable concept support sizes even under naive statistical filtering.\noccupy aligned directions. DTN therefore selects the de-\ncoder atom closest to the concept text embedding. This is\nsimple and training-free, but assumes that concepts local-\nize to single sparse features and that text-side alignment\nidentifies the same features used by corresponding audio\nexamples. Both assumptions can fail: concepts may be dis-\ntributed across neurons, and text and audio embeddings re-\nmain separated by a modality gap [26,29,30]. Multimodal\nSAEs can also exhibit split dictionaries with modality-\nspecific features [18,20], so text-aligned atoms may not be\nactivated by concept-bearing audio. Thus, text alignment\nalone does not imply faithful audio-side support.\n3. METHOD AND OBSERVATIONS\n3.1 SAE Training\nWe train SAEs on audio embeddings from JamendoMax-\nCaps [32]. Audio clips are encoded with the audio towers\nfA of CLAP [28] and MuQ [33]; from each 10-second\nframe we extract an embedding za ∈R512.\nWe train\nBatchTopK SAEs 3 [2] with m = 4096 latent features (ex-\npansion factor 8), sparsity levels L0 ∈{5, 10, 20, 50, 100},\nand decoder D instantiated as a linear layer with weights\ntied to the encoder. All models are trained for 50k steps us-\ning AdamW (learning rate = 10−4) on a single L40 GPU.\n3.2 Concepts Appear as Neuron Bundles\nWe first examine the decoder geometry induced by DTN\n(Section 2.2). Using an internal music-tag taxonomy, we\ncollect the top 50 tags in each of the categories Genres,\nMoods, Instruments, Usage, and Era, yielding roughly 300\nshort-form concepts. For each concept c with text embed-\nding zc, we compute cosine similarities between zc and\nevery decoder atom di. We then fit a Gaussian to each\nconcept-specific similarity distribution and define a DTN\nsupport K(c) by retaining atoms whose similarity exceeds\nthe fitted mean by more than four standard deviations. This\n3 We also experimented with TopK [5] and Matching Pursuit SAE [4],\nwith similar results overall. Here, we focus on BatchTopK SAE, the most\nwide\n\n=== PAGE 4 ===\nsparsity as Π is the same sparsifying operator used during\nSAE training. Unlike cosine probing, which ranks neu-\nrons independently, Eq. (3) selects neurons because they\njointly reconstruct the target concept direction under the\naudio dictionary. The resulting code is therefore a sparse\nexplanation of the concept in the basis actually used for\nsteering retrieval.\nIn practice, unconstrained inversion can drift towards\ntext-side directions that do not lie on the audio man-\nifold [34, 35].\nTo bias the solution toward plausible\naudio embeddings, we add a distributional regularizer\nΓ(A, D(Π(u))), implemented as a Mahalanobis distance\nwith respect to an empirical audio embedding distribu-\ntion A estimated from 10k test-set MaxCaps audio embed-\ndings. Our full objective is therefore:\nu⋆\nc = arg min\nu\n\u0010\ndcos\n\u0010\nzc, D(Π(u))\n\u0011\n+ γ Γ(A, D(Π(u)))\n\u0011\n,\n(4)\nwhere γ > 0 controls manifold adherence to prevent un-\nconstrained drift. An alternative to the Γ term would be\nto optimize through the full audio encoder without setting\nany distributional prior, as in [34]. However, this would\nmake the inversion process prohibitively slow for inter-\nactive steerable retrieval (∼20s / inversion on CPU). We\nhence adopt the more lightweight Mahalanobis regulariza-\ntion proposed in [35], yielding runtimes compatible with\nreal-world applications (∼20ms / inversion on CPU).\nWe\noptimize\nEq.\n4\nvia\ngradient\ndescent\nusing\nAdam [36], but observed convergence issues, which we\nsolve by initializing u from the sparse code of the near-\nest audio neighbour to the text embedding zc. Alterna-\ntively, taking advantage of the linearity of the decoder D,\nwe propose optimizing Eq. 4 using FISTA, a fast algorithm\ndesigned for solving linear inverse problems [37]. We re-\nfer to these variants as Adam inversion and FISTA inver-\nsion. A hyperparameter sweep showed that both methods\nremain stable across a range of configurations, and we fix\nγ = 10−4 in all subsequent experiments.\nFinally, to guard against the influence of overly generic\nfrequent neurons, we apply an IDF-weighted penalty after\noptimization [20,22] (for fairness of comparison with DTN\nprobing, to which we apply the same IDF-weighing):\nsk ←wksk,\nwk = log\nN\ndf(k) + ϵ,\n(5)\nwhere df(k) counts the number of audio samples for which\nneuron k is active.\nFrequent neurons therefore incur\nstronger sparsity pressure. The final support is obtained by\noptimizing Eq. 4, applying the IDF weighting in Eq. 5, and\ntaking the top active coordinates. After optimization, we\nuse the same weighting to rank the recovered coordinates\nand define the concept support K(c) as the active neurons\nin the optimized and weighted sparse code.\nThis yields a training-free concept-to-support mapping:\ninstead of ranking atoms independently by text similarity,\ninversion selects a sparse set of atoms that jointly recon-\nstructs the concept while staying near the audio distribu-\ntion. In the following experiments, we compare these sup-\nports against cardinality-matched DTN supp\n\n=== PAGE 5 ===\nOriginal caption\nThis is a slow-paced, ambient electronic\ntrack in C major with a tempo of around\n89 BPM, featuring a synth and a 4/4\ntime signature.\nAlternative views\nAdam inversion\nThe music is instrumental with elements\nof ambient, electronic and soundtrack\ngenres. It creates an inspiring mood\nand utilizes instruments such as the\npiano, synthesizer, and violin. The time\nsignature is 4/4 with a tempo of 80 bpm.\nDTN probing\nThe music is an instrumental piece with\ncinematic elements, predominantly\nfeaturing strings in Fm key with a tempo\nof 120bpm.\nFISTA inversion\nThe music in question is an instrumental\npiece predominantly featuring piano with\nan additional layer of synth strings. It\nfalls under ambient, classical and\ncontemporary, exuding a calm, melodic,\nrelaxing, and soft mood. The rhythm\nmaintains a steady tempo at 77bpm.\nFigure 4: Retrieving ambient electronic music with amplified “piano”. Starting from an audio embedding z, we\nnavigate on the hypersphere by amplifying the concept “piano” with strength α. Left: PCA projections of the trajectories\nobtained with different methods as α increases. The original caption (α = 0) and captions of nearest neighbour to the edited\nembeddings (α = 0.3) are shown, with correct and drifting attributes highlighted. Sphere densities represent the audio\n(A) and text (T ) manifolds. Right: Evolution of the preservation and editing metrics as α increases, for K ∈{5, 10, 20}.\nthe recovered bundle is active in positive examples, and\nBundleHitRate(c), the fraction of positive examples with\nnonzero overlap (see Figure 3).\n4.2 Results\nFigure 3 shows that cosine-probed supports perform poorly\nboth structurally and functionally. Although the selected\natoms may look semantically plausible under decoder–text\nsimilarity, they overlap weakly with positive audio sup-\nports, produce many zero-overlap cases, and give weak\nconcept discrimination. This suggests that cosine prob-\ning often finds atoms that are near the concept in decoder\nspace, but not the sparse support used by concept-bearing\naudio examples.\nInverse supports close this gap.\nUnder cardinality-\nmatched comparisons, they yield higher bundle hit rate,\n10–70% better bundle recall across sparsities, and substan-\ntially better probe performance (∼50% AUROC →∼65–\n80%). Thus, inversion does not only produce a more plau-\nsible concept bundle; it recovers supports that are more of-\nten active in positive audio examples and more informative\nfor detecting the concept.\nTaken together, these results provide a first structural\nvalidation of the inverse formulation. DTN appears to re-\ncover semantic association in decoder space, but not faith-\nful support localization in the sparse audio representation.\nInversion, by contrast, recovers supports that are more\naligned with the neurons activated by positive examples\nand more informative for concept detection. This supports\nour central claim that concept attribution in multimodal\nsparse spaces should be treated as a geometric in\n\n=== PAGE 6 ===\nquery. Let c be the target concept, z the original embed-\nding, z′ the edited embedding, and NNK(z) the top-K au-\ndio neighbours of z in the 10k MaxCaps test set.\nFor target change, we use three metrics. First, we mea-\nsure whether the edited query retrieves more neighbours\ntagged with c:\n∆ρc@100 = ρc@100(z′) −ρc@100(z),\n(8)\nwhere ρc@K(z) is the fraction of NNK(z) tagged with c.\nSecond, we report the change in probability assigned to c\nby an audio tag classifier,\n∆ˆpclf(c) = ˆpclf(c | z′) −ˆpclf(c | z).\n(9)\nFinally, as a text-alignment diagnostic, we report\n∆CLAP = cos(z′, zc) −cos(z, zc), where zc is the text\nembedding of the concept.\nFor preservation, we measure whether the edit stays\naudio-like and whether it changes unrelated tags. Audio-\nlikeness is measured by pA(z′), from a held-out audio-\nvs-text classifier. Off-target drift is measured with a co-\noccurrence-weighted Jaccard score over non-target neigh-\nbour tags, reported as ∆Jco\nother@100. Tags that naturally\nco-occur with c are downweighted using dataset-level tag\nco-occurrence. This avoids penalizing plausible changes\nsuch as adding electric guitar to a query and retrieving\nmore rock. We also report |∆Γ(z, A)|, the change in Ma-\nhalanobis distance to the empirical audio distribution, but\ntreat it as a diagnostic as the same distance is used in the\ninversion objective.\n5.3 Results\nFigure 4 shows an example of amplifying the “piano” con-\ncept with α ∈{0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0}. All methods im-\nprove the target concept as α increases, but inversion does\nso more efficiently: at matched α and support size K, it\nmoves the edited embedding closer to the concept while\ncausing less preservation metric degradation. Both inverse\nvariants also remain markedly closer to the audio manifold\nin Mahalanobis distance, suggesting that the recovered edit\ndirections are better aligned with audio embeddings. In\nother words, DTN can produce sensible edits, but it often\ndoes so by moving toward the text manifold.\nFigure 5 shows edit–preservation trade-offs over the top\n20 MaxCaps concepts. For each method, we collect all edit\nsettings (c, K, α) and estimate an upper frontier using the\n90th percentile edit score within bins of the preservation\nmetric, retaining the top 10% of outcome. We then apply a\nlight smoothing spline only for visualization.\nAcross metrics, all methods exhibit the expected trade-\noff between stronger edits and greater preservation cost.\nThe inverse methods occupy better high-score frontiers\nthan cosine probing: at matched off-target drift or modality\nadherence, they achieve larger target-neighbour and classi-\nfier gains. This indicates that audio-side inverse supports\nare not only better aligned structurally, but translate into\nstronger retrieval edits at comparable preservation levels.\nPoor regimes still exist for some (c, K, α) settings, sug-\ngesting limits from concept ambiguity, embedding-space\nquality, or SAE reconstruction fidelity.\ngood suppress\ngood add\n-0.5\n0.0\n0.5\nNeighbour ∆ρc@\n\n=== PAGE 7 ===\n7. ACKNOWLEDGEMENT\nThis work is supported by the EPSRC UKRI Centre for\nDoctoral Training in Artificial Intelligence and Music\n(EP/S022694/1) and Universal Music Group.\n8. AI USAGE STATEMENT\nAI LLM assistants were used sparingly in the development\nof this project to accelerate the implementation of evalu-\nation pipelines. AI assistance was also used sparingly in\nwriting this paper to tighten phrasing, reword for conci-\nsion, and smooth out mathematical notation consistency.\n9. REFERENCES\n[1] S.\nRajamanoharan,\nT.\nLieberum,\nN.\nSonnerat,\nA. Conmy, V. Varma, J. Kramár, and N. Nanda,\n“Jumping ahead:\nImproving reconstruction fidelity\nwith jumprelu sparse autoencoders,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2407.14435, 2024.\n[2] B. Bussmann, P. Leask, and N. Nanda, “Batchtopk\nsparse autoencoders,” in NeurIPS 2024 Workshop on\nScientific Methods for Understanding Deep Learning,\n2024.\n[3] B. Bussmann, N. Nabeshima, A. Karvonen, and\nN. Nanda, “Learning multi-level features with ma-\ntryoshka sparse autoencoders,” in International Con-\nference on Machine Learning.", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": f